  <div class="neo-card neo-panel neo-reading" style="max-width:850px;margin:0 auto 65px">
   <p>AI assistants are changing. Instead of just answering questions, they can now complete tasks, use apps, and keep working in the background.</p>
   <p>Three interesting products are <strong>Grok Bot, Meta Muse, and OpenAI Dots</strong>. Here's how they compare.</p>
   <h2>What are they?</h2>
   <h3>Grok Bot — xAI</h3>
   <p>Grok Bot works more like a digital teammate. You can assign it tasks, and it can use tools and applications to get things done.</p>
   <p>It's particularly interesting for developers, researchers, and people who want help with technical work.</p>
   <h3>Meta Muse — Meta</h3>
   <p>Muse focuses more on everyday life. It can help with planning, bookings, messages, and other personal tasks.</p>
   <p>It runs in its own cloud environment and can connect with services you already use.</p>
   <h3>OpenAI Dots — OpenAI</h3>
   <p>Dots are designed for ongoing work. They remember useful context, connect with apps, and can make progress on tasks without needing repeated instructions.</p>
   <p>For example, a Dot could follow a project, research updates, or help prepare reports over time.</p>
   <h2>Quick comparison</h2>
   <div style="overflow-x:auto"><table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;text-align:left">
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Feature</th><th scope="col">Grok Bot</th><th scope="col">Meta Muse</th><th scope="col">OpenAI Dots</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
     <tr><th scope="row">Company</th><td>xAI</td><td>Meta</td><td>OpenAI</td></tr>
     <tr><th scope="row">Main focus</th><td>Technical work</td><td>Personal tasks</td><td>Ongoing work</td></tr>
     <tr><th scope="row">Background tasks</th><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
     <tr><th scope="row">App integrations</th><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
     <tr><th scope="row">Cloud computer</th><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
     <tr><th scope="row">Best for</th><td>Developers</td><td>Individuals</td><td>Professionals and teams</td></tr>
    </tbody>
   </table></div>
   <h2>Which one would I choose?</h2>
   <p>As a developer, <strong>Grok Bot and OpenAI Dots interest me more</strong>.</p>
   <p>Grok Bot looks useful for assigning technical tasks, while Dots seems better suited to work that continues over several days or involves multiple applications.</p>
   <p>Muse has a different focus. I can see it being useful for everyday activities, but it's not where I'd start for software development.</p>
   <p>One thing I'd like to test is how reliably these agents complete tasks without human intervention. A good demo is one thing. Getting consistent results on real projects is another.</p>
   <h2>What about pricing?</h2>
   <p>All three have different access models.</p>
   <ul>
    <li><strong>Grok Bot:</strong> Available through eligible subscriptions.</li>
    <li><strong>Meta Muse:</strong> Free access for some uses, with paid options.</li>
    <li><strong>OpenAI Dots:</strong> Available through eligible ChatGPT plans.</li>
   </ul>
   <p>Exact prices and usage limits depend on the plan and rollout.</p>
   <h2>Final thoughts</h2>
   <p>I don't think there's a clear winner yet.</p>
   <p>Grok Bot looks promising for technical tasks, Muse for personal assistance, and Dots for ongoing professional work.</p>
   <p>What matters to me is simple: <strong>Can I give an agent a task, trust it to make progress, and review the result without constantly checking on it?</strong></p>
   <p>That's what I'll be watching as these products evolve.</p>
